WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:04.330
He-Man is back and he still has the power!

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:08.210
Now that "Masters of the Universe: Revelation"
is out on Netflix, many viewers will want

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:11.769
to look back at the original show for more
muscle-bound action.

00:00:11.769 --> 00:00:17.439
We're here to catch you up on lore and anything
else you may have forgotten about.

00:00:17.439 --> 00:00:22.419
When most people think of He-Man it's about
muscles, action, and the fights against Skeletor

00:00:22.419 --> 00:00:27.449
to save Eternia . Each episode creates a new
conflict that sends the hero into battle to

00:00:27.449 --> 00:00:32.309
help someone or keep the peace — but that
can't be all that the titular character does

00:00:32.309 --> 00:00:34.280
with his time, right?

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:38.719
He-Man was eventually given an alternate identity
in the form of Prince Adam within the pages

00:00:38.719 --> 00:00:39.899
of the comics.

00:00:39.899 --> 00:00:44.850
This was kept a secret from many of the supporting
cast, so that Skeletor couldn't simply kill

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:47.140
his foe while he wasn't powered up.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:51.679
But it also gave the character a weakness
and created some entertaining story moments

00:00:51.679 --> 00:00:57.659
when Adam couldn't easily slip away to transform,
or had to make excuses to exit a battle so

00:00:57.659 --> 00:00:59.829
his alter ego could help out.

00:00:59.829 --> 00:01:05.220
During the classic show's episodes, viewers
are given a few small glimpses into Adam's

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:10.740
duties as a member of the royal family: attending
events like the joust, and watching performances

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:15.890
from Man-E-Faces and other entertainers, as
well as other small diplomatic outings.

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:20.590
When those duties aren't taking up his free
time, the Prince is seen training, or more

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:26.820
often opting to skip these lessons with Teela
to nap or enjoy other leisure activities.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:31.240
For a series that takes place on Eternia,
everyone would be forgiven for assuming there

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:34.840
were no Earthlings in the story, but that's
not true.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:39.490
The show sees its fair share of people from
the planet, mostly in the form of kids — as

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:41.180
we saw in the Christmas special.

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:46.740
However, a couple of members from the main
cast are hiding that secret origin as well.

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:51.570
Queen Marlena, He-Man's mother, was originally
an astronaut from Earth who was thrown off

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:55.189
course after meteors hit her ship, the Rainbow
Explorer.

00:01:55.189 --> 00:02:00.390
This detour to Eternia was supposed to last
a few days, but after we are shown a flashback

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:05.750
of this in the episode "The Rainbow Warrior,"
she explains that love took hold instead.

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:11.000
This sequence might have meant more had she
not dropped a random, "back on Earth," line

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:15.200
during the joust earlier, but it's still an
intriguing revelation.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:19.720
Marlena gets to show off her piloting skills
and save the day in this adventure, while

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:23.910
Prince Adam is stuck without his powers, temporarily
at least.

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:30.250
This means that Prince Adam is also half-Earthling,
and even he — as He-Man — is shocked to

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:35.000
see that it was his mother that freed them,
meaning it is unlikely many other than King

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:38.600
Randor knew much of her past as a captain
of the stars.

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:42.890
This is also the episode where Queen Marlena
not-so-subtly hints that she knows who her

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:44.720
son truly is.

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:51.840
"Adam, a mother always knows her own son and
what he is capable of doing."

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:57.400
His name is a cringe-inducing attempt at a
pun, but the backstories for the comics tried

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:03.090
to make "Man-E-Faces" more serious, giving
him the origin of a tortured actor, transformed

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:06.810
by one of Skeletor's potions and given split
personalities.

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:11.481
The character would make only two main appearances
and a couple of cameos in the show, leaving

00:03:11.481 --> 00:03:14.930
more of an imprint with his gimmick than time
on screen.

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:18.790
He was originally going to pop in for a side
gag in the episode "Into the Abyss," but was

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:20.700
cut to help the tone.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:26.290
Those who do recall Man-E-Faces remember him
as a hero, fighting alongside He-Man, or at

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:30.380
least are aware of his reputation as the greatest
actor in Eternia.

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:34.810
But in "The Mystery of Man-E-Faces," viewers
are also shown that he began on the wrong

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:40.380
side of the law, terrorizing people smaller
than him, even if he wasn't purely evil.

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:45.020
Man-E-Faces tells He-Man that these acts were
because others were afraid of him, fearful

00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:47.340
of his abilities, and shunned him for this.

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:53.510
In his own words, this led him to be "cruel"
toward everyone and steal from them.

00:03:53.510 --> 00:03:58.400
After a run-in with Skeletor and Beast Man,
who abused him for their own purposes, He-Man

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:04.290
and Teela helped free the future actor, and
Orko even gave Man-E-Faces his name.

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:08.990
Teela is arguably one of the most interesting
characters in the He-Man universe, but having

00:04:08.990 --> 00:04:13.460
several changes made to her backstory and
altering the relationship she shares with

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:17.789
the group for various incarnations can make
it difficult to keep up with her.

00:04:17.789 --> 00:04:22.759
In the original mini comics, Teela was a clone
of the Sorceress created by Skeletor, an element

00:04:22.759 --> 00:04:27.440
that was probably inserted to help explain
the armor on the original action figure.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:30.460
In other versions, Man-at-Arms is her biological
father.

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:35.960
There's also a theory that Fisto might actually
be her dad, and in the 1983 cartoon Duncan

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:40.979
adopts Teela and keeps the identity of her
mother a secret — which is revealed in the

00:04:40.979 --> 00:04:42.819
episode "Teela's Quest."

00:04:42.819 --> 00:04:47.690
Here, Teela gets a bit envious of Prince Adam's
relationship with Queen Marlena and sets off

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:51.819
to find out who her mother really is by seeking
the oracle.

00:04:51.819 --> 00:04:57.740
Being a bit rash, Teela heads off to a dangerous
place by herself and runs afoul of Mer-Man

00:04:57.740 --> 00:05:00.909
and a sea monster, but He-Man shows up to
help.

00:05:00.909 --> 00:05:05.600
She goes through all of this and discovers
that her mother is none other than the Sorceress,

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:08.669
only to have those memories erased.

00:05:08.669 --> 00:05:13.611
The knowledge is treated as an incredibly
important tidbit that she can't know, but

00:05:13.611 --> 00:05:16.240
now He-Man is aware of it also.

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:20.830
This is brought up one other time in "Teela's
Triumph," where she temporarily assumes the

00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:23.259
role of the sorceress.

00:05:23.259 --> 00:05:26.030
Many viewers were not fond of Orko.

00:05:26.030 --> 00:05:29.340
This could be because the character didn't
appear in the initial run of the toy line

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:31.819
— having been created for the cartoon.

00:05:31.819 --> 00:05:35.820
Or perhaps it's because he typically played
the role of comedy relief.

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:38.699
He is the King's Royal Buffoon, after all.

00:05:38.699 --> 00:05:43.990
Orko was a klutz, never really able to do
his magic correctly, often hindering the heroes

00:05:43.990 --> 00:05:47.520
on their missions and constantly damaging
Man-at-arms' lab.

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:51.720
Somehow, he's still one of Prince Adam's closest
allies.

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:56.919
It isn't surprising that most fans were more
upset at the lack of Battle Cat in the live-action

00:05:56.919 --> 00:05:58.810
film than not seeing Orko.

00:05:58.810 --> 00:06:03.270
He's a powerful character – even if he has
trouble getting his spells to work properly.

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:09.509
As a Trollan, Orko possesses a mastery over
matter creation, his hat opens up into a dimensional

00:06:09.509 --> 00:06:14.850
space where he can keep any number of items,
as well as an extra hand comes out of there,

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:19.300
and his teleportation powers allow him to
easily escape many traps.

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:23.620
His potent tricks do seem to work somewhat
better back in his home dimension, where the

00:06:23.620 --> 00:06:25.810
laws of physics work differently.

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:30.240
There is a medallion that helps him as well,
but some of these points contradict themselves

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:31.669
as the show progresses.

00:06:31.669 --> 00:06:37.639
More importantly, in "Creatures from the Tar
Swamp," Orko saves Adam and Cringer at a young

00:06:37.639 --> 00:06:40.529
age, before they had their powers.

00:06:40.529 --> 00:06:44.800
He-Man was originally supposed to wield a
battle axe back when he was in toy form, but

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:49.490
looking back now, he wouldn't seem right without
his Power Sword.

00:06:49.490 --> 00:06:55.069
The "Masters of the Universe" toys also came
with two swords, and these had to be put together

00:06:55.069 --> 00:06:57.100
to unlock Castle Grayskull.

00:06:57.100 --> 00:07:01.629
In the '80s, however, shows came under fire
from parent groups and politicians alike,

00:07:01.629 --> 00:07:05.999
prepared to blame much of society's ills on
the pop culture of the day.

00:07:05.999 --> 00:07:11.751
As pre-emptive measures, Skeletor got a staff,
He-Man held back on his swordplay, and other

00:07:11.751 --> 00:07:16.810
creative decisions were made to walk a tightrope
ensuring that the series could be kid-friendly,

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:19.529
yet still be appealing to the older crowd
as well.

00:07:19.529 --> 00:07:20.529
"Teela!

00:07:20.529 --> 00:07:23.909
Could you put your finger here for a second?"

00:07:23.909 --> 00:07:28.939
He-Man barely uses his fists, let alone his
sword, and most of his aggressive actions

00:07:28.939 --> 00:07:32.150
are levied against large monsters or robots.

00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:37.330
Whenever the hero is dealing with humanoid-type
characters, his tactics become more about

00:07:37.330 --> 00:07:38.999
subduing the enemies.

00:07:38.999 --> 00:07:43.729
His preferred method is usually to throw large
rocks, or simply to pick the perpetrator up

00:07:43.729 --> 00:07:45.569
and toss them.

00:07:45.569 --> 00:07:51.050
Public Service Announcements — often shortened
to PSAs — have a long and complicated history

00:07:51.050 --> 00:07:54.270
that can arguably be traced back to the Civil
War.

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:58.460
Filmation is often mentioned for modernizing
these messages with their work on "Fat Albert

00:07:58.460 --> 00:08:00.439
and the Cosby Kids" and "He-Man."

00:08:00.439 --> 00:08:04.720
The latter almost had to have these little
lessons due to how closely parents related

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:09.499
the show to violence, and the producers tried
hard to balance out the program.

00:08:09.499 --> 00:08:14.699
Running 130 episodes, the show covered a lot
of ground with these messages, so it makes

00:08:14.699 --> 00:08:17.400
sense that they don't all land quite right.

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:20.960
A few are uncomfortable and others are just
plain silly.

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:26.150
Consider, for instance, the PSA that had Ram
Man telling kids not to run their heads into

00:08:26.150 --> 00:08:27.150
things.

00:08:27.150 --> 00:08:31.839
"Trying to use your head the way I do's not
only dangerous; it's dumb."

00:08:31.839 --> 00:08:37.070
Others were a bit more serious, and included
a wide variety of pertinent topics, such as:

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:42.120
inappropriate touching, ageism, judging people
based on their appearances, not taking shortcuts

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:46.650
for wealth and power, accepting failures and
learning from mistakes.

00:08:46.650 --> 00:08:51.520
Within the episodes themselves were some solid
examples of needing to have faith and that

00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:56.290
even He-Man can lose his confidence, which
the creators say paid off based on stories

00:08:56.290 --> 00:08:59.250
fans told them years later.

00:08:59.250 --> 00:09:03.460
It's no secret that Filmation was banking
on "He-Man and the Masters of the Universe"

00:09:03.460 --> 00:09:07.240
to help put the company on the map, and to
make sure everything worked out the way they

00:09:07.240 --> 00:09:10.100
wanted it to, costs had to be kept low.

00:09:10.100 --> 00:09:15.850
This pride in an American-made show kept under
budget led to cuts all around, with voice

00:09:15.850 --> 00:09:19.100
acting and animation taking the largest hits.

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:24.460
As anyone who watches the show can hear, various
small characters have familiar voices, and

00:09:24.460 --> 00:09:30.620
zoomed-in shots frequently take the place
of actual animation, as reused assets, rewound

00:09:30.620 --> 00:09:33.649
motions, and replayed sequences became the
norm.

00:09:33.649 --> 00:09:38.500
Speaking of cutting corners, the character
of Orko originally had another name, Gorpo,

00:09:38.500 --> 00:09:42.210
which can still be heard in some Portuguese
translations of the show.

00:09:42.210 --> 00:09:47.250
This late alteration seems to come from a
financial standpoint instead of a creative

00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:51.620
one, because if the character had a large
letter G imprinted on his clothing, it would

00:09:51.620 --> 00:09:55.950
be harder to flip footage of him and make
scenes look different without having to redraw

00:09:55.950 --> 00:09:57.500
the floating magician.

00:09:57.500 --> 00:10:02.500
This brings into question how many other changes
might have happened all in the name of saving

00:10:02.500 --> 00:10:06.950
a few dollars, even if Orko is the better
name.

00:10:06.950 --> 00:10:12.130
Most folks don't think of great writing or
production value when recalling "He-Man and

00:10:12.130 --> 00:10:16.240
the Masters of the Universe," but the truth
is that the program had some incredibly skilled

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:18.130
behind-the-scenes talent.

00:10:18.130 --> 00:10:22.490
Not only was there a tremendous group of voice
actors who brought the characters to life

00:10:22.490 --> 00:10:26.700
– many of them having to talk to themselves
in a majority of the scenes – but other

00:10:26.700 --> 00:10:30.940
voices had to be filled in by one of the founders
of Filmation, Lou Scheimer.

00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:36.370
Additionally, his wife and daughter even lent
a hand, making it quite a tight-knit group.

00:10:36.370 --> 00:10:41.470
The energetic music was handled by Haim Saban
and Shuki Levy , giants with stellar resumes

00:10:41.470 --> 00:10:43.320
in the world of children's programming.

00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:48.520
Behind the scenes was Bruce Timm, now known
as the mind behind some of DC Comics' greatest

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:49.900
animated projects.

00:10:49.900 --> 00:10:54.190
One of his earliest gigs was as a background
artist and designer for "He-Man," while he

00:10:54.190 --> 00:10:56.790
was also given a chance to work with the mini-comics.

00:10:56.790 --> 00:11:01.030
Some of his fellow comic book brethren were
present to help on scripts, with Paul Dini

00:11:01.030 --> 00:11:04.680
and J. Michael Straczynski working on the
show early in their careers.

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:08.980
"Star Trek" scribe D.C. Fontana contributed
an episode featuring the origin of Battle

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:10.230
Cat as well.

00:11:10.230 --> 00:11:15.110
Even if children's show trappings restricted
what could be done, no one could ever say

00:11:15.110 --> 00:11:19.050
that He-Man didn't have a wealth of talent
behind him.

00:11:19.050 --> 00:11:24.470
"Masters of the Universe: Revelation" is attempting
to give fans a proper continuation to the

00:11:24.470 --> 00:11:28.360
'80s cartoon, but it's not the first time
this has been attempted.

00:11:28.360 --> 00:11:33.450
Although rarely remembered, there was an attempt
at a direct sequel that mutated into its own

00:11:33.450 --> 00:11:34.890
strange oddity.

00:11:34.890 --> 00:11:40.310
Sales for the toy line had begun to decline
drastically by 1986, and it was evident that

00:11:40.310 --> 00:11:43.360
the franchise needed some sort of rejuvenation.

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:48.170
This came in the form of a redesigned set
of toys, which would require a new show to

00:11:48.170 --> 00:11:50.100
help sell them like last time.

00:11:50.100 --> 00:11:52.870
Enter "The New Adventures of He-Man."

00:11:52.870 --> 00:11:57.890
This show uprooted Adam from Eternia, placing
him instead on the ultra-high-tech planet

00:11:57.890 --> 00:11:59.040
Primus.

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:04.470
Leaving most of the cast behind, Skeletor
did tag along to remain his nagging nemesis.

00:12:04.470 --> 00:12:10.200
This near-complete rehaul of He-Man featured
a protagonist that didn't look like the musclebound

00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:15.760
warrior kids were accustomed to seeing, as
well as one of the worst designs for Skeletor.

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:21.600
Other than the upgraded visuals, almost everything
else was worse, and the show is often remembered

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:24.530
— if at all — as the weakest of the franchise.

00:12:24.530 --> 00:12:29.360
Somehow, "New Adventures" ran for 65 episodes.

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:33.890
There were rumors of a second season that
would send Adam back to Eternia, but instead

00:12:33.890 --> 00:12:37.520
the '90s led He-Man into relative obscurity.

00:12:37.520 --> 00:12:42.230
With "Masters of the Universe: Revelation,"
perhaps it is finally time once again for

00:12:42.230 --> 00:12:45.550
He-Man to have the power.

00:12:45.550 --> 00:12:47.600
